[saved output to C:\Users\aryan\Documents\GitHub\AA-AI465\lab5\outputs\exp5_cot_selfconsistency.txt]</pre><br style='page-break-before:always'><p class=cap>Figure 1. The three configurations by accuracy (left) and by model calls (right). Accuracy and cost are plotted on separate panels rather than against two y-axes on one. Generated by make_plots.py from this run's own results.</p><img src='outputs/exp5_fig1_comparison.png' style='width:100%;'><p class=cap>Figure 2. The vote tally for every question at k=7, split by whether each vote went to the gold answer, with greedy's outcome on the same question marked beneath the axis. Bars totalling fewer than 7 votes are questions where a sampled trace was unparsable and therefore discarded before the vote.</p><img src='outputs/exp5_fig2_tallies.png' style='width:100%;'><br style='page-break-before:always'><h2>Observations</h2><h3>1. Which questions self-consistency corrects that greedy gets wrong, and what their vote tallies have in common</h3><p>CoT greedy scores 7/10 (accuracy 0.70), getting q05, q07 and q09 wrong because its single fixed path takes exactly the plausible-looking shortcut built into each of those three questions (treating the markup and discount as cancelling out, rounding the half day up, and an off-by-one on the station count). Self-consistency at k=7 scores 9/10 (accuracy 0.90), correcting q07 (tally 2600:4, 2800:1) and q09 (tally 8:6, 9:1) but not q05 (tally 80:4, 75:3). What the two corrected tallies have in common is a clear plurality for the correct answer &mdash; the wrong shortcut is still sampled, but only once out of seven traces in both cases, so the correct path's traces dominate the vote. q05 is the case that stays wrong, and its tally is exactly the one question here where the wrong shortcut (4 votes) actually outnumbers the correct path (3 votes) &mdash; self-consistency can only recover the right answer when correct traces are the plurality, not automatically on every question.</p><h3>2. What a narrow winning margin tells you about the answer that won</h3><p>q05's tally of 80:4 against 75:3 out of seven traces is the narrowest margin in the whole run, and it is also the one question where the vote winner is wrong. A narrow margin means the sampled traces were nearly evenly split between two (or more) distinct reasoning paths, which is precisely the situation where the plurality answer is least trustworthy &mdash; a small change in which traces happened to be sampled, or a slightly different seed, could easily have flipped the winner (Exercise 1's per-seed rows for k=1 and k=3 show exactly this kind of run-to-run swing). A wide margin, by contrast (for example q03 and q04's unanimous 6/6 votes, or q01 and q09's 6:1 splits), means almost every sampled reasoning path agreed, which is a much stronger indication that the winning answer reflects the question's actual structure rather than a close coin flip between two competing shortcuts.</p><h3>3. Model calls spent per additional correct answer</h3><p>Direct and CoT greedy each spend 10 calls (one per question) for 7 correct answers. Self-consistency at k=7 spends 70 calls for 9 correct answers &mdash; 60 additional calls for 2 additional correct answers relative to greedy, i.e. 30 calls per additional correct answer at this k. Exercise 1's sweep makes the same trade-off explicit as k grows: mean accuracy rises from 0.660 at k=1 to 0.960 at k=9 while total calls (summed over 5 seeds) rise from 50 to 450, and accuracy stops improving appreciably beyond k=9 (k=15 also reaches 0.960 but at 750 calls) &mdash; so the calls spent per correct answer keep climbing well after the accuracy gain has flattened out, which is the direct cost of self-consistency's k-times-the-calls price.</p><h3>4. Why unparsable traces must be counted separately from wrongly-answered traces</h3><p>The self-consistency run above extracts a numeric answer from 65 of the 70 sampled traces and finds no parseable &ldquo;Final Answer:&rdquo; line in the other 5 &mdash; those 5 are excluded from every vote tally entirely rather than being counted as votes for a wrong answer. Collapsing the two categories would corrupt the measurement in two directions at once: folding unparsable traces into &ldquo;wrong&rdquo; would understate the model's reasoning accuracy by blaming it for an output-formatting failure it may not have made, while silently dropping them without reporting the count would hide a real, separate failure mode &mdash; a model that reasons correctly but states its answer in an unexpected form is a formatting problem to fix in the prompt or the extractor, not a reasoning problem to fix by sampling more traces. Reporting them separately is also what makes the vote tallies interpretable: a tally that sums to less than k is immediately legible as k minus however many traces the extractor discarded.</p><br style='page-break-before:always'><h2>Exercise 1 &mdash; Sweeping k over {1, 3, 5, 9, 15}</h2><p><b>Task.</b> Sweep k over 1, 3, 5, 9 and 15, repeating each setting with several different seeds, and report accuracy and total model calls for each k. State the k beyond which accuracy stops improving appreciably, and the calls spent per correct answer at that point.</p><p><b>Approach.</b> Each k in {1, 3, 5, 9, 15} is run against the full question set with five different seeds (11, 22, 33, 44, 55); mean accuracy is averaged over the five seeds and total calls is summed over them (k &times; 10 questions &times; 5 seeds).</p><h3>Program &mdash; exp5_exercises.py</h3><pre class=code style='font-size:7.5pt'>&quot;&quot;&quot;
Experiment 5 - Exercises

Exercise 1: Sweep k over {1, 3, 5, 9, 15}, repeating each setting with
five different seeds, and report mean accuracy and total model calls per
k. State the k beyond which accuracy stops improving appreciably and the
calls spent per correct answer at that point.

Exercise 2: Add an abstention rule to the self-consistency vote: answer
only if the winning answer&#x27;s vote count leads the runner-up by at least
`margin` votes; otherwise abstain. Report coverage and accuracy-on-
answered-subset for two margins.
&quot;&quot;&quot;

import os

from questions import QUESTIONS
from exp5_cot_selfconsistency import (
    generate_trace,
    extract_answer,
    majority_vote,
    canonical_number,
    DEFAULT_SEED,
)

OUT_DIR = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(__file__), &quot;outputs&quot;)
os.makedirs(OUT_DIR, exist_ok=True)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Exercise 1: sweep k, multiple seeds
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

K_VALUES = [1, 3, 5, 9, 15]
SEEDS = [11, 22, 33, 44, 55]


def run_self_consistency(questions, k, seed):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Same logic as run(&#x27;cot_self_consistency&#x27;, ...) in the main script,
    factored out here so the sweep can call it once per (k, seed) pair
    without re-importing run() and re-deriving the config name.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    correct_count = 0
    total_calls = 0
    unparsable_count = 0
    for q in questions:
        gold = canonical_number(q[&quot;gold&quot;])
        answers = []
        for i in range(k):
            trace_text, _ = generate_trace(q[&quot;id&quot;], &quot;sample&quot;, sample_index=i, seed=seed)
            total_calls += 1
            ans = extract_answer(trace_text)
            if ans is None:
                unparsable_count += 1
            else:
                answers.append(ans)
        winner, _tally = majority_vote(answers)
        if winner == gold:
            correct_count += 1
    accuracy = correct_count / len(questions)
    return accuracy, total_calls, unparsable_count


def run_exercise1(emit):
    emit(f&quot;k values: {K_VALUES}&quot;)
    emit(f&quot;seeds per k: {SEEDS}&quot;)
    emit()

    summary = []
    for k in K_VALUES:
        accuracies = []
        calls_per_seed = []
        for seed in SEEDS:
            acc, calls, unparsable = run_self_consistency(QUESTIONS, k, seed)
            accuracies.append(acc)
            calls_per_seed.append(calls)
            emit(f&quot;  k={k:&lt;3} seed={seed:&lt;4} accuracy={acc:.2f}  calls={calls}  unparsable={unparsable}&quot;)
        mean_acc = sum(accuracies) / len(accuracies)
        total_calls = sum(calls_per_seed)
        summary.append((k, mean_acc, total_calls))
        emit(f&quot;  -&gt; k={k}: mean accuracy over {len(SEEDS)} seeds = {mean_acc:.3f}, &quot;
             f&quot;total model calls across those seeds = {total_calls}&quot;)
        emit()

    emit(&quot;=== Summary: mean accuracy and total calls per k ===&quot;)
    emit(f&quot;{&#x27;k&#x27;:&lt;6}{&#x27;mean accuracy&#x27;:&lt;16}{&#x27;total calls&#x27;:&lt;14}&quot;)
    for k, mean_acc, total_calls in summary:
        emit(f&quot;{k:&lt;6}{mean_acc:&lt;16.3f}{total_calls:&lt;14}&quot;)
    emit()

    # Identify the k beyond which accuracy stops improving appreciably:
    # the first k whose mean accuracy is within 0.01 of the best mean
    # accuracy achieved by any larger or equal k in the sweep.
    best_acc = max(a for _, a, _ in summary)
    plateau_k = None
    for k, mean_acc, total_calls in summary:
        if best_acc - mean_acc &lt;= 0.01:
            plateau_k = k
            plateau_calls = total_calls
            plateau_correct = mean_acc * len(QUESTIONS)
            break
    emit(f&quot;Accuracy plateaus (within 0.01 of the best mean accuracy &quot;
         f&quot;{best_acc:.3f}) starting at k={plateau_k}.&quot;)
    calls_per_correct = plateau_calls / plateau_correct
    emit(f&quot;At k={plateau_k}: total calls (summed over {len(SEEDS)} seeds) = &quot;
         f&quot;{plateau_calls}, mean correct answers per seed = &quot;
         f&quot;{plateau_correct:.2f}/{len(QUESTIONS)}, &quot;
         f&quot;so calls spent per correct answer (single seed) = &quot;
         f&quot;{plateau_k * len(QUESTIONS) / plateau_correct:.2f}.&quot;)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Exercise 2: abstention by vote margin
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Margin is defined as an absolute vote-count difference between the
# winning answer and the runner-up (votes, not a fraction of k): the
# system answers only if winner_votes - runnerup_votes &gt;= margin.
# A question with only one distinct extracted answer has no runner-up,
# treated as a runner-up count of 0, so it always clears any margin
# tested here (a unanimous vote is the strongest possible signal).

MARGINS = [1, 2]


def vote_with_abstention(tally, margin):
    if not tally:
        return None, &quot;abstain&quot;
    ranked = sorted(tally.items(), key=lambda x: -x[1])
    winner, winner_votes = ranked[0]
    runnerup_votes = ranked[1][1] if len(ranked) &gt; 1 else 0
    if winner_votes - runnerup_votes &gt;= margin:
        return winner, &quot;answered&quot;
    return None, &quot;abstain&quot;


def run_exercise2(emit, k=7, seed=DEFAULT_SEED):
    emit(f&quot;Margin definition: absolute vote-count lead of the winner over &quot;
         f&quot;the runner-up (winner_votes - runnerup_votes &gt;= margin). &quot;
         f&quot;k={k}, seed={seed}.&quot;)
    emit()

    for margin in MARGINS:
        emit(f&quot;--- margin={margin} ---&quot;)
        answered = 0
        correct_on_answered = 0
        for q in QUESTIONS:
            gold = canonical_number(q[&quot;gold&quot;])
            answers = []
            for i in range(k):
                trace_text, _ = generate_trace(q[&quot;id&quot;], &quot;sample&quot;, sample_index=i, seed=seed)
                ans = extract_answer(trace_text)
                if ans is not None:
                    answers.append(ans)
            _winner_plain, tally = majority_vote(answers)
            decision, status = vote_with_abstention(tally, margin)
            if status == &quot;answered&quot;:
                answered += 1
                is_correct = (decision == gold)
                correct_on_answered += int(is_correct)
                mark = &quot;OK&quot; if is_correct else &quot;WRONG&quot;
                emit(f&quot;  {q[&#x27;id&#x27;]}: tally={dict(tally)}  -&gt; ANSWERED {decision}  gold={gold}  [{mark}]&quot;)
            else:
                emit(f&quot;  {q[&#x27;id&#x27;]}: tally={dict(tally)}  -&gt; ABSTAIN  gold={gold}&quot;)

        coverage = answered / len(QUESTIONS)
        acc_on_answered = (correct_on_answered / answered) if answered else float(&quot;nan&quot;)
        emit(f&quot;  coverage={coverage:.2f} ({answered}/{len(QUESTIONS)})  &quot;
             f&quot;accuracy_on_answered={acc_on_answered:.2f} &quot;
             f&quot;({correct_on_answered}/{answered})&quot; if answered else
             f&quot;  coverage={coverage:.2f} ({answered}/{len(QUESTIONS)})  &quot;
             f&quot;accuracy_on_answered=n/a (nothing answered)&quot;)
        emit()


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Driver
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def main():
    lines = []

    def emit(s=&quot;&quot;):
        print(s)
        lines.append(s)

    emit(&quot;=== Exercise 1: sweep k over {1, 3, 5, 9, 15}, 5 seeds each ===&quot;)
    run_exercise1(emit)

    emit()
    emit(&quot;=== Exercise 2: abstention by vote margin ===&quot;)
    run_exercise2(emit)

    out_path = os.path.join(OUT_DIR, &quot;exp5_exercises.txt&quot;)
    with open(out_path, &quot;w&quot;) as f:
        f.write(&quot;\n&quot;.join(lines) + &quot;\n&quot;)
    print(f&quot;[saved output to {out_path}]&quot;)


if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:
    main()</pre><br style='page-break-before:always'><h3>Output</h3><pre class=out style='font-size:6.5pt'>=== Exercise 1: sweep k over {1, 3, 5, 9, 15}, 5 seeds each ===
k values: [1, 3, 5, 9, 15]
seeds per k: [11, 22, 33, 44, 55]

  k=1   seed=11   accuracy=0.40  calls=10  unparsable=1
  k=1   seed=22   accuracy=0.70  calls=10  unparsable=0
  k=1   seed=33   accuracy=0.70  calls=10  unparsable=0
  k=1   seed=44   accuracy=0.80  calls=10  unparsable=0
  k=1   seed=55   accuracy=0.70  calls=10  unparsable=0
  -&gt; k=1: mean accuracy over 5 seeds = 0.660, total model calls across those seeds = 50

  k=3   seed=11   accuracy=0.80  calls=30  unparsable=3
  k=3   seed=22   accuracy=0.90  calls=30  unparsable=2
  k=3   seed=33   accuracy=0.90  calls=30  unparsable=0
  k=3   seed=44   accuracy=0.90  calls=30  unparsable=2
  k=3   seed=55   accuracy=0.80  calls=30  unparsable=1
  -&gt; k=3: mean accuracy over 5 seeds = 0.860, total model calls across those seeds = 150

  k=5   seed=11   accuracy=0.60  calls=50  unparsable=3
  k=5   seed=22   accuracy=1.00  calls=50  unparsable=5
  k=5   seed=33   accuracy=0.90  calls=50  unparsable=2
  k=5   seed=44   accuracy=0.90  calls=50  unparsable=3
  k=5   seed=55   accuracy=0.80  calls=50  unparsable=2
  -&gt; k=5: mean accuracy over 5 seeds = 0.840, total model calls across those seeds = 250

