[saved output to C:\Users\aryan\Documents\GitHub\AA-AI465\lab5\outputs\exp5_exercises.txt]</pre><p class=cap>Figure 3. Mean accuracy over the five seeds against k (left, with each seed's individual accuracy shown as a faint point behind the mean) and total model calls against k (right). The per-seed spread is what makes the mean dip slightly at k=5; k is drawn on evenly spaced ticks, so the cost line bends even though calls are exactly k &times; 10 questions &times; 5 seeds.</p><img src='outputs/exp5_fig3_k_sweep.png' style='width:100%;'><h3>Discussion</h3><p>Mean accuracy rises steadily and noisily at small k (0.660 at k=1, with individual seeds ranging from 0.40 to 0.80, showing how unstable a single sample or a small sample is) up through 0.860 at k=3 and 0.840 at k=5, before reaching 0.960 at k=9 and staying at 0.960 at k=15 &mdash; so k=9 is the point beyond which accuracy stops improving appreciably in this run; doubling k to 15 buys no further mean accuracy at all, only 300 more calls. At k=9 the mean number of correct answers per seed is 9.60/10, for 90 calls per seed, giving 90 / 9.60 &asymp; 9.38 calls per correct answer at the plateau &mdash; noticeably worse than the 10/7 &asymp; 1.43 calls per correct answer of greedy CoT, which is the concrete cost of buying the extra ~2 correct answers that self-consistency recovers on this question set. The mean also masks real per-seed variance (k=5 dips to 0.60 accuracy on seed 11, worse than several k=1 seeds), which is exactly why the exercise asks for several seeds rather than reporting a single run's number as if it were k's true accuracy.</p><br style='page-break-before:always'><h2>Exercise 2 &mdash; Abstention by vote margin</h2><p><b>Task.</b> Add an abstention rule that answers only when the winning answer leads the runner-up by at least a stated margin, and otherwise declines to answer. Report coverage and accuracy on the answered subset for two different margins, and discuss the trade-off.</p><p><b>Approach.</b> Margin is defined as an absolute vote-count lead of the winning answer over the runner-up (<span class=m>winner_votes - runnerup_votes &ge; margin</span>); a question with only one distinct extracted answer is treated as having a runner-up count of 0, so a unanimous vote always clears any margin tested here. Two margins are tested at k=7, seed=42: margin=1 (answer whenever the winner has any lead at all) and margin=2 (require a lead of at least two votes).</p><p class=cap>Figure 4. Coverage and accuracy on the answered subset at each margin. Raising the margin from 1 to 2 drops one question (q05, the 4&ndash;3 split) out of the answered set, which is precisely the question the vote got wrong.</p><img src='outputs/exp5_fig4_abstention.png' style='width:70%;'><h3>Discussion</h3><p>At margin=1, coverage is 1.00 (10/10 questions answered) with accuracy-on-answered of 0.90 (9/10) &mdash; identical to the unconstrained self-consistency result, since every question in this run has at least a 1-vote lead for its plurality answer. At margin=2, the rule abstains on exactly q05 &mdash; the same question flagged in Observation 2 as having the narrowest margin in the whole run (80:4 vs 75:3, a lead of only 1 vote) &mdash; dropping coverage to 0.90 (9/10) but raising accuracy-on-answered to a perfect 1.00 (9/9). The trade-off is therefore visible in a single concrete case: tightening the margin from 1 to 2 sacrifices 10% coverage but removes precisely the one question the system was actually going to get wrong, because a narrow margin and a wrong answer coincided here. That is not a guarantee &mdash; a narrow margin only signals higher uncertainty in the vote, not certain wrongness &mdash; but it shows abstention buys a genuine accuracy improvement on the questions it still answers, at the cost of leaving the harder questions unanswered rather than answering them wrong.</p><br style='page-break-before:always'><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>The chain-of-thought self-consistency pipeline was implemented end to end on a fixed ten-question set: two prompt templates (Direct and worked-example CoT), a scripted per-question reasoner that renders a correct path and one or two plausible wrong paths, a regex-based <span class=m>extract_answer()</span> that normalises numeric answers to a canonical form and returns <span class=m>None</span> on unparseable traces, and a majority vote with a documented tie-break rule. Direct and CoT-greedy both scored 7/10 at 10 model calls each, since greedy's single fixed path takes the plausible-but-wrong shortcut on three questions built specifically to contain one; self-consistency at k=7 scored 9/10 at 70 calls, correcting two of those three by plurality vote while its remaining error coincided with the single narrowest vote margin observed anywhere in the run.</p><p>The two exercises then quantified that trade-off directly. Sweeping k over {1, 3, 5, 9, 15} across five seeds each showed mean accuracy climbing from 0.660 to a 0.960 plateau reached at k=9, at a marginal cost of about 9.38 calls per correct answer at that plateau versus roughly 1.43 for greedy CoT &mdash; the price of self-consistency's k-times-the-calls scaling made concrete. Adding an abstention rule keyed on the winning margin then showed that the question self-consistency still gets wrong is exactly the one with the thinnest vote margin: tightening the margin requirement from 1 to 2 votes dropped coverage from 1.00 to 0.90 but raised accuracy on the answered subset from 0.90 to a perfect 1.00, by declining to answer precisely the question the vote was least confident about.</p><h2>Execution environment and declaration</h2><table class=g><tr><th style='width:22%'>Item</th><th style='width:78%'>Detail</th></tr><tr><td>Execution</td><td>Python 3.12.1 on Windows (AMD64), executed 24 September 2026, 19:29</td></tr><tr><td>Files</td><td>questions.py, exp5_cot_selfconsistency.py, exp5_exercises.py, make_plots.py, make_report.py</td></tr><tr><td>Libraries used</td><td>Python standard library only (<span class=m>random</span>, <span class=m>re</span>, <span class=m>collections.Counter</span>) for every program that produces a result; Matplotlib (permitted, Table 1) is used solely by <span class=m>make_plots.py</span> to draw the figures, and influences no reported number &mdash; it imports the same functions the console programs call and plots their return values. No agent framework, orchestration library, or external API/network access was used, in accordance with clause 3.1 of the Rules and Regulations.</td></tr><tr><td>Reproducibility</td><td>The question set and gold answers are fixed, every sampled trace is drawn from a <span class=m>random.Random</span> seeded deterministically from (seed, question id, sample index), and the greedy path per question is fixed, so every run at a given seed and k reproduces exactly.</td></tr><tr><td>Sources consulted</td><td>Course manual for Experiment 5 (chain-of-thought prompting, self-consistency by sampling, majority voting over extracted answers).</td></tr></table><p class=note>All output reproduced above was produced by executing the programs listed in this record on the machine and at the time stated in the table.</p></div></body>
